3. §4 known boundaries: add bullet block for SPIKE-001-FIX-002:
```
- SPIKE-001-FIX-002 scheme B (native drag) semantics and known boundaries:
  - Per §9.1 (human review takes precedence), the `DRAG_B_MODE` constant default is `"on_press"`: call `window_start_drag()` inside the button press event;
    `"on_threshold"` (call only after exceeding 6px) is only an experimental timing, validity is judged manually in GUI, and must not be assumed valid by default.
  - Scheme B cannot preserve the exact same 6px threshold semantics (§9.2 explicit documentation obligation): A mode = "only start drag when displacement reaches 6px";
    B mode = "OS immediately takes over on press, on end judge click (net displacement <6px) or drag by net displacement". In both modes the constant `DRAG_THRESHOLD_PX=6.0` is unchanged.
  - The OS may swallow the release event → `_process`'s left-button mask polling fallback (`native_drag_end` + position save + report write); on crash, the final drag position may not be saved, honest record.
  - If in GUI measurement scheme B does not take over the window, or produces focus/occlusion anomalies: keep scheme A (A/B are independent, key 4 can always be switched back, §9.3), the judgment of "native drag is incompatible" is submitted to manual, no silent scheme change or requirement degradation.
  - §9.5: `Input.use_accumulated_input` (default true) merges mouse motions per frame, is one cause capping A-mode window position update at 30Hz; key 6 toggles for experiments,
    and the 30 FPS canonical default is not touched at all (key 7 only affects runtime FPS control).
  - Headless exit=0 only proves that the script/scene can be loaded without error; whether the native drag is smoother, whether click semantics is preserved, whether position saves normally is all manually accepted in GUI (§9.6).
```

